# ESP32 Sensor Server — Progress Log

## What We Built Today

A WiFi-connected ESP32 that reads sensor data and serves it as JSON over HTTP,
discoverable on the local network by name using mDNS.

---

## Architecture

```
ESP32 (sensor node)
  ├── connects to WiFi
  ├── starts mDNS broadcast (esp32_cam1.local)
  ├── starts HTTP server on port 80
  │     └── GET /sensors → returns JSON
  └── loop: reads sensors every 5s, updates shared data with mutex

Python (laptop/Pi)
  └── GET http://esp32_cam1.local/sensors → receives JSON
```

---

## Arduino Libraries Used

| Library | Purpose | Installation |
|---------|---------|-------------|
| `WiFi.h` | Connect ESP32 to WiFi network | Built-in with ESP32 board package |
| `ESPAsyncWebServer` | HTTP server with async request handling | Arduino Library Manager — version 3.x by ESP32 Async |
| `ArduinoJson` | Build and serialize JSON responses | Arduino Library Manager |
| `ESPmDNS` | Broadcast device name on local network | Built-in with ESP32 board package |

---

## Key Concepts Learned

### I2C
- Two-wire protocol (SDA + SCL) for talking to sensors
- Each device has a unique address (BH1750: 0x23, SCD40: 0x62, BMP280: 0x76)
- Master (ESP32) initiates, slaves (sensors) respond
- Always run I2C scanner first to verify wiring before writing sensor code

### SPI
- Four-wire protocol (MOSI, MISO, SCK, CS)
- Full duplex — sends and receives simultaneously
- CS pin selects which device to talk to
- Write-only devices (displays) don't need MISO
- ST7789 display needs DC (Data/Command) pin in addition to SPI pins
- 3.3V logic level required — needs level shifter with 5V Arduino Uno

### HTTP Server on ESP32
- `ESPAsyncWebServer` runs in a background FreeRTOS task
- `server.on("/path", HTTP_GET, handler)` registers an endpoint
- Use `vTaskDelay` instead of `delay()` to avoid blocking background tasks
- Server starts once in `setup()`, handles requests automatically

### mDNS
- Broadcasts device name on local network (e.g. `esp32_cam1.local`)
- Resolves name to IP automatically — IP can change, name stays fixed
- ESP32 side: `MDNS.begin(name)` + `MDNS.addService("http", "tcp", 80)`
- Python side: use name instead of IP in URL
- Requires devices to be on the same network
- Does NOT work on networks with AP isolation enabled

### Mutex (FreeRTOS)
- Needed because server handler and main loop run in different FreeRTOS tasks
- Both access shared sensor data — race condition without protection
- `SemaphoreHandle_t mutex` declared globally, initialised in `setup()`
- Must be initialised in `setup()`, not at global scope — FreeRTOS not ready yet
- `xSemaphoreTake` to lock, `xSemaphoreGive` to unlock

---

## AP Isolation — Important Gotcha
University/office/hotel WiFi often enables AP isolation, which blocks
device-to-device communication on the same network. Symptoms:
- ESP32 gets an IP and connects to WiFi successfully
- Serial Monitor works fine
- But HTTP requests never reach the ESP32
- Fix: use a home network or mobile hotspot where AP isolation is disabled
- Document this requirement in your project README

